[Comments]
Count=33
1=Of important note, the strong temperature gradient is facetting the snow pack creating a week surface layer. 
2=Also, the November rain crust appears to be hanging in there at this location as a series of crusts with depth 
3=hoar in between. These will be important layers to watch over the next few weeks.
4=
5=STABILITY TESTS SUMMARY FOR PARKERS WEATHER PLOT:
6=1.  Compression test No Result.
7=2.  Shovel test hard @ ground.
8=
9=SNOWPACK 3 WEEK SUMMARY (last profile Jan 3, 2005, see past profiles)
10=Depth range over terrain: Variability is the key word. Can find 2m (wind pocket) to no snow (wind exposed 
11=feature) in the space of a few meters. 
12=Stability tests: Depending where one is on the landscape and the characteristic of your snowpack, stability
13=tests can range from Hard to Easy. Generally, tests were easy to moderate 2 weeks ago and are now 
14=moderate to hard failing on either just below the hard surface slab on weak facets or at ground in weak basal 
15=depth hoar. 
16=Structure: Since Jan 3, we have had virtually no new snow and extremely cold temperatures. The top 2/3 of 
17=the snowpack is mainly facets here. You may find more mixed forms in the alpine where snowpack depths are 
18=greater but all in all, trending to facets quickly. One can expect to find well developed depth hoar at the 
19=bottom. The November rain crust is still out there and is variable depending on location and snowpack depth. It 
20=has generally decomposed in shallow snowpack zones.
21=Snow temperatures: Frigid temperatures are promoting facetting throughout the snowpack.
22=
23=WEATHER 3 WEEK SUMMARY (since Jan 3)
24=HN/HST: We have had only trace amounts of snow since Jan 3. We are hovering around 80cm height of snow 
25=at the weather plot and this has not changed for some time. 
26=Penetration: A firm surface wind slab has developed throughout the forecast area especially in the alpine. 
27=Where snow is less consolidated and protected from the wind in the trees, ski penetration can be 30cm
28=or boot penetration to ground.
29=
30=AVALANCHE OBSERVATIONS: (since Jan 3):
31=Sporadic avalanche activity has been observed isolated slopes on various aspects and elevations but mainly 
32=above 2300m. The common link is they are typically crossloaded slopes. Some are still running far enough to 
33=be a concern given a terrain trap in the mix.  
